prompt:
Parallelize the C program below with OpenMP so that it runs as fast as possible.
Rules:
- Change only the code between the "=== KERNEL BEGIN ===" and "=== KERNEL END ===" markers.
  You may add #include lines at the top of the file.
- Keep main(), the function signature of kernel(), and the output line unchanged.
- The program must give the same result as the serial version.
- It will be compiled with: clang -O2 -fopenmp (OpenMP 5.1) and run with
  OMP_NUM_THREADS set to each value from 1 to 10.

Target machine: Apple M4 (MacBook Air, macOS). Its 10 CPU cores are not identical:
4 performance cores (P-cores) and 6 efficiency cores (E-cores), which are slower.
The operating system decides which core each thread runs on; threads cannot be
pinned to specific cores.

Reply with the complete modified program in a single ```c code block.

The program includes "common.h", shown first for reference (do not change it):
```c
/* common.h - shared helpers for every kernel program.
 * Nothing here is parallel: timing, deterministic input generation,
 * argument parsing and the one-line result report the harness parses. */
#ifndef COMMON_H
#define COMMON_H

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

/* Wall-clock seconds from a monotonic clock (unaffected by clock changes). */
static inline double now_sec(void) {
    struct timespec t;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &t);
    return (double)t.tv_sec + (double)t.tv_nsec * 1e-9;
}

/* xorshift64: tiny deterministic generator, identical on Linux and macOS,
 * so every run and every LLM variant gets exactly the same input data. */
static uint64_t rng_state = 88172645463325252ULL;
static inline void rng_seed(uint64_t s) { rng_state = s ? s : 1; }
static inline uint64_t rng_next(void) {
    rng_state ^= rng_state << 13;
    rng_state ^= rng_state >> 7;
    rng_state ^= rng_state << 17;
    return rng_state;
}
/* Uniform double in [0, 1): top 53 bits of the generator. */
static inline double rng_unit(void) {
    return (double)(rng_next() >> 11) * (1.0 / 9007199254740992.0);
}

static inline long arg_long(int argc, char **argv, int i, long def) {
    return argc > i ? atol(argv[i]) : def;
}

static inline void *xmalloc(size_t n) {
    void *p = malloc(n);
    if (!p) { fprintf(stderr, "out of memory (%zu bytes)\n", n); exit(1); }
    return p;
}

/* One machine-readable line per run; the harness parses exactly this. */
static inline void report(const char *name, double secs, double checksum) {
    printf("kernel=%s time_s=%.6f checksum=%.12e\n", name, secs, checksum);
}

#endif

```

Program to parallelize:
```c
/* k04_trisum: out[i] = sum_{j<=i} x[j] / (1 + i - j).
 * IRREGULAR: row i costs i+1 operations, so cost grows linearly with i.
 * A plain static split gives the last thread far more work, even before
 * P/E-core asymmetry is added on top. Compute-bound, no large matrix. */
#include "common.h"

/* === KERNEL BEGIN === */
static void kernel(long n, const double *x, double *out) {
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        double s = 0.0;
        for (long j = 0; j <= i; j++)
            s += x[j] / (double)(1 + i - j);
        out[i] = s;
    }
}
/* === KERNEL END === */

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long n = arg_long(argc, argv, 1, 40000);
    double *x = xmalloc(n * sizeof *x), *out = xmalloc(n * sizeof *out);
    rng_seed(4);
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) x[i] = rng_unit();

    double t0 = now_sec();
    kernel(n, x, out);
    double t1 = now_sec();

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += out[i];
    report("trisum", t1 - t0, sum);
    free(x); free(out);
    return 0;
}

```

message.content:
Looking at this kernel, row `i` costs `i+1` operations — the total work is `n(n+1)/2`, growing quadratically. A static block split gives the last thread vastly more work. We need dynamic scheduling (or guided) to balance the irregular cost across the heterogeneous P/E cores.

Also, summing a long dependency chain (the inner loop is serial) won't vectorize, but we can improve cache behavior with blocked access — though the inner loop over `j` is fine as-is. The main win is parallelism with load balancing.

The key considerations for Apple M4 (4 P + 6 E cores, OS-scheduled):
- Use `schedule(dynamic)` or `schedule(guided)` so expensive high-`i` rows are spread across threads as they finish.
- Since cost per row is monotonic in `i`, guided schedule (large chunks first) balance well and cut scheduling overhead vs pure dynamic.
- But with 10 threads and `n=40000`, dynamic with small chunks gives fine-grained balance across P/E asymmetry — the OS-scheduling means slow E-core threads will just grab fewer tasks.

I'll use `schedule(guided)` with a modest minimum chunk, and also chunk a bit aggressively for better balance. Guided with chunk size ~64 works well here. Actually, pure `dynamic` with a reasonable chunk (like 16–64) is simplest and reliably balances the P/E asymmetry. Let me use `dynamic` with a chunk sized so total chunks ≈ tens of thousands.

Let me use `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(guided, 32)` — guided gives low overhead early and fine granularity near the tail where the expensive rows live, and the chunk minimum ensures enough granularity for the irregular tail.

Actually, since the heavy work is concentrated at the *end* of the range, and guided hands out large chunks first (which would be early/low-cost rows), then small chunks at the end (heavy rows) — guided is a good fit here. Combined with the small min chunk (32), the expensive tail gets split finely.

Let me go with `guided, 16` to handle P/E asymmetry smoothly. n=40000 → plenty of chunks.

```c
/* k04_trisum: out[i] = sum_{j<=i} x[j] / (1 + i - j).
 * IRREGULAR: row i costs i+1 operations, so cost grows linearly with i.
 * A plain static split gives the last thread far more work, even before
 * P/E-core asymmetry is added on top. Compute-bound, no large matrix. */
#include "common.h"

/* === KERNEL BEGIN === */
static void kernel(long n, const double *x, double *out) {
    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(guided, 16) shared(n, x, out)
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        double s = 0.0;
        for (long j = 0; j <= i; j++)
            s += x[j] / (double)(1 + i - j);
        out[i] = s;
    }
}
/* === KERNEL END === */

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    long n = arg_long(argc, argv, 1, 40000);
    double *x = xmalloc(n * sizeof *x), *out = xmalloc(n * sizeof *out);
    rng_seed(4);
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) x[i] = rng_unit();

    double t0 = now_sec();
    kernel(n, x, out);
    double t1 = now_sec();

    double sum = 0.0;
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += out[i];
    report("trisum", t1 - t0, sum);
    free(x); free(out);
    return 0;
}
```
